=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 has an over-restrictive iptables firewall rule injected into its FORWARD chain that silently drops all traffic arriving on eth2 (the WAN-facing interface to router3, which carries all inter-site traffic from Location A). The full FORWARD chain reads: (1) ACCEPT TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, (2) DROP TCP from eth2, (3) DROP UDP from eth2, (4) DROP all protocols from eth2 (the injected catch-all). Rules 1–3 were already present as scoped access-list policy; rule 4 (`-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`) is the injected fault. Because rule 4 uses no `-p` flag it matches every protocol including ICMP, blocking the 4 ICMP echo-request packets from the pc4→laptop4 ping that were confirmed dropped (4 pkts / 336 bytes counted on that rule after the test). The routing plane itself is healthy: all seven routers have RIP running with standard 30/180/120 timers, a complete chain of permanent static (kernel) routes exists on every hop from router0 through router6 for 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24, and router6's RIP database shows consistently refreshed routes back to Location A with ~3 minutes remaining on each timer. The user's perception of "routes aging out" is a mischaracterisation of silent packet drops — traffic routes correctly to router6 but is discarded before forwarding. The intermittency arises because rule 1's specific HTTP exception (172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.1:80) lets some application traffic through while all other probes (ping, UDP, general TCP) fail. Corrective fix: remove the injected catch-all rule with `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` on router6.
=== END ===